00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.630
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.280
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.060
I am Priyanka.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.180
And in this episode, we'll talk
about Warm Disaster Recovery

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.098
pattern for applications that
are deployed on Google Cloud.

00:00:13.098 --> 00:00:17.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.250
Main Street Art currently
runs their web application

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.600
on Compute Engine.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:27.980
It's mostly a static website.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.370
So in the unlikely event that
their site is not available,

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.130
they can use a failover to
static website on Google Cloud

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.420
Storage.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.760
Main Street Art is
very cost-conscious,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.250
so they have gone for
a zonal setup, which

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.220
won't cause them problems if
the zone becomes temporarily

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.150
unavailable.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.390
So this recipe provides them
with a cost-effective way

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:48.560
to recover.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.040
Their primary application
runs on Google Compute Engine.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.760
These instances are grouped
into managed instance groups.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.540
And the instance groups serve as
backend services for HTTPS load

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.770
balancer.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.560
The HTTP load balancer
directs incoming traffic

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.960
to the instances according
to the load balancer

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.020
configuration, the configuration
of each instance group,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.250
and the health of each instance.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.660
This provides HA, but for
the belt and braces approach,

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.610
this recipe is worth discussing.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.080
Here's how Main Street
Art would set it up.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.440
They would have to
create a VPC network

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.750
and then create a custom
image that's configured

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.760
with the application service.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.790
Use that image to create
an instance template.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.940
Using this instance
template, configure

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.980
a managed instance group
with health checks and load

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:34.970
balancing.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.390
Then create a Cloud
Storage-based static website.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.790
Check out earlier
episodes to cook it up.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.740
Then finally,
configure the Cloud

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.710
DNS to point to the
primary application,

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.890
and the standby static
site sits dormant.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.860
Now, what happens in case the
Compute Engine application

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.700
goes down?

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.310
Well, Main Street
Art would just have

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.850
to configure Cloud DNS to
point to the static site.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.240
And when Compute Engine is back
up and running again and can

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.610
support production
workloads, Main Street Art

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.650
can reverse the recovery steps.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.710
They will just have to configure
Cloud DNS to point to the load

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.750
balancer that fronts
the instances.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.230
Well, there you have it.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.310
If your web application
already runs on Google Cloud

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.530
and you are on this
very specific budget

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.440
to meet those RTO
and RPO values, then

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.200
you can use the Warm
DR pattern and failover

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.840
to a static website on
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.270
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.720
Here's hoping you're whipping
up your own DR strategy.

